  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday: A ridge of high pressure maintains influence over the\nInland Northwest through Wednesday, keeping temperatures warm in\nthe mid to upper 90s. A Heat Advisory remains in effect through\nWednesday evening for the Lewiston-Clarkston Valley, where\ntemperatures will climb into the upper 90s to triple digits.\nWesterly winds pick up through the Cascade gaps Wednesday\nafternoon and evening as an approaching system pushes cool \nmarine air onshore west of the Cascades. Combined with afternoon\nhumidity values in the low to mid 20s, these breezy winds will\nresult in elevated fire weather conditions across Central WA.\nIn addition to breezy and dry conditions across Central WA,\nthere is potential (around a 15 percent chance) for isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms across the Northern Mountains and \nNorth ID Wednesday afternoon as mid- level moisture increases, \ntemperatures cool aloft, and a weak shortwave disturbance \nsupplies synoptic scale lift. Convective- allowing models aren't\noverly enthusiastic with most only indicating a cell or two \ndeveloping over the course of the evening, so confidence is not\nhigh that storms will materialize, but the potential is there.\nAny storms that do develop may bring gusty outflow winds due to\na dry sub-cloud layer.\n\nThursday: A trough and associated cold front will sweep across \nthe region late Wednesday night into early Thursday, tightening \nthe pressure gradient across the Northwest and bringing gusty \nwesterly winds by Thursday afternoon. Sustained winds of 15-25 \nmph with gusts of 30-40 mph are expected. Temperatures Thursday \nwill be 9-12 degrees cooler compared to Wednesday, but humidity \nvalues will still be low in the 20-25 percent range. The \ncombination of stronger winds with low relative humidity values \nwill be enough to create elevated to locally critical fire \nweather conditions. A Fire Weather Watch has been issued for the\nOkanogan Valley and Waterville Plateau Thursday afternoon and \nevening where the strongest winds and lowest RH values are \nexpected.\n\nFriday through Tuesday: Below normal temperatures return for the\nweekend with chances for mountain showers. Winds will be breezy\nat times, but the cooler temperatures look to keep relative \nhumidity levels above critical thresholds. /Fewkes",
